WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.300
Why Finpoint?

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.800
So you’ve decided to take charge of your spending, maybe after noticing your bank balance running dry faster than you expected.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:10.370
Welcome to Finpoint.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:16.590
This guide walks you through everything Finpoint can do, so you can use all of it, or just the parts you need.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.460
Track several accounts, or just a few daily payments from one.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.520
Finpoint works either way.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:26.244
But your banking app already lists every payment, so what’s missing?

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.550
Your bank can tell you where every dollar went.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.930
What it can’t tell you is what each payment was really for.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.550
Let’s say you spent $80 at the supermarket:

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:41.420
$50 on groceries, and $30 on a birthday gift for a friend.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:45.520
Your banking app shows one $80 payment, and nothing more.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:50.718
But to you, those are two very different things, and you’d like to label them that way.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.840
Or let’s say you paid $90 for dinner with a friend, and they sent their $60 share back a week later.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:05.440
Your banking app shows two payments, a week apart, so you’d have to scroll past everything in between to get the full picture.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.879
But to you, it’s really one $30 dinner, and you’d like to label it that way.

00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:14.063
Finpoint was built to close that gap.

00:01:14.423 --> 00:01:20.023
It keeps your bank activity exactly as your bank reports it, and lets you label it in your own words:

00:01:20.273 --> 00:01:22.203
groceries, $50.

00:01:22.443 --> 00:01:24.173
A gift, $30.

00:01:24.313 --> 00:01:26.493
Your share of dinner, $30.

00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:29.357
That’s the whole idea of Finpoint:

00:01:29.637 --> 00:01:34.367
adding labels to your bank activity, one or many, simple or detailed.

00:01:34.797 --> 00:01:37.903
Once your spending is labelled, it finally makes sense.

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.357
So how do you use Finpoint?

00:01:40.637 --> 00:01:42.607
It comes down to just two things:

00:01:42.697 --> 00:01:44.987
bring in your bank activity, and label it.

00:01:45.317 --> 00:01:47.500
Everything else falls into place from there.

00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:50.587
To do that, you’ll need three ideas.

00:01:50.817 --> 00:01:51.717
Statements:

00:01:51.827 --> 00:01:54.997
your bank activity, exactly as your bank reports it.

00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:56.197
Records:

00:01:56.257 --> 00:01:58.017
your labels for that activity.

00:01:58.177 --> 00:01:59.397
And Allocations:

00:01:59.597 --> 00:02:01.037
the links between the two.

00:02:02.667 --> 00:02:04.981
Statements, Records and Allocations

00:02:06.081 --> 00:02:13.391
A Statement is one line of bank activity, exactly as your bank reports it, like that $80 supermarket payment.

00:02:13.831 --> 00:02:19.251
Finpoint never lets you edit the Statements your bank gives you, so you can trust them to match your bank.

00:02:19.611 --> 00:02:21.351
Well, almost always.

00:02:21.511 --> 00:02:22.458
We’ll get to that.

00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:29.984
A Record is your label for what happened, like the groceries for $50, and the gift for $30.

00:02:30.867 --> 00:02:36.297
An Allocation links a Statement to a Record, and says how much of the Statement belongs to it.

00:02:36.757 --> 00:02:43.743
Here, $50 of the supermarket payment is allocated to Groceries, and the other $30 to the gift.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:47.470
So one Statement can be split across several Records.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:53.019
That’s how you untangle a mixed shopping trip, or a bill that covers two different things.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.510
It works the other way too:

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.580
several Statements can be combined into one Record.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:04.800
Your $90 dinner payment and your friend’s $60 transfer both belong to one Dinner Record.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.950
It shows what you really spent:

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:08.221
$30.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.150
Most of the time, though, it’s simpler:

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:13.900
one Statement, labelled by one Record.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.030
A $12 lunch you had on your own is one Statement, and one Lunch Record.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.525
No need to overcomplicate things.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.340
And when things get messier, Finpoint keeps up.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:32.940
Say you bought $80 of groceries and a $40 gift in one checkout, but paid $60 on each of two cards.

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:37.498
That’s two Statements and two Records, linked by three Allocations.

00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:41.017
We won’t need anything that tangled in this guide.

00:03:41.337 --> 00:03:45.107
It’s just to show that Finpoint is as flexible as you need it to be.

00:03:45.397 --> 00:03:46.597
Enough examples.

00:03:46.847 --> 00:03:49.296
Let’s get you familiar with Finpoint itself.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.419
Trying Finpoint first

00:03:53.519 --> 00:03:56.789
On your first visit, you’ll be greeted by an empty Dashboard.

00:03:57.119 --> 00:04:00.459
Since you don’t have any data yet, choose Try demo data.

00:04:00.859 --> 00:04:06.805
It loads three bank accounts and five months of Records, so you can see what a lived-in Finpoint looks like.

00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:12.527
When you’re done exploring, clear the demo from the Data page, with Clear all data.

00:04:12.917 --> 00:04:15.502
Then you’re ready to import your own bank activity.

00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:18.828
Importing your bank activity

00:04:19.928 --> 00:04:26.078
Many banks let you download your activity as a file, called an export, from their website or app.

00:04:26.508 --> 00:04:31.938
Finpoint doesn’t connect to your bank directly, so this file is how your bank activity gets in.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.790
Import the file exactly as your bank gave it to you.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:41.942
Finpoint reads each bank’s export as it is, so an edited file might not import correctly.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:53.562
For now, Finpoint reads exports from DBS, OCBC, UOB and Revolut, as a CSV file or an Excel spreadsheet, depending on the bank.

00:04:54.433 --> 00:04:56.733
You don’t need to sort your files by account.

00:04:57.143 --> 00:05:03.813
Each export says which bank account it came from, so Finpoint files its Statements under a matching Account for you.

00:05:04.283 --> 00:05:11.159
Import exports from two DBS accounts, and you’ll get two Accounts in Finpoint, each with its own Statements.

00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:13.723
Revolut is the exception:

00:05:14.063 --> 00:05:16.873
its exports don’t say which account they came from.

00:05:17.243 --> 00:05:24.660
For those, Finpoint will ask you which Account the file belongs to, or to create a new one, with an ID and a name.

00:05:25.533 --> 00:05:27.173
Let’s import your first file.

00:05:27.513 --> 00:05:33.479
Download a CSV or Excel export from your bank, once for each account you want to track.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.640
Then open the Importer from the sidebar, choose your bank, and add your files.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:44.031
You can add several at once, except for Revolut, which takes one file at a time.

00:05:44.733 --> 00:05:48.970
Choose Import, and Finpoint will tell you how many new Statements came in.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:54.700
And as we mentioned, a Revolut export needs one more choice before you import it:

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:58.890
pick its Account, or create a new one with an ID and a name.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.626
Your new Statements now wait in Workflows for you to label, so that’s where we’re heading next.

00:06:06.233 --> 00:06:07.225
Workflows

00:06:08.325 --> 00:06:12.995
Workflows is your to-do list, and it’s where you’ll spend most of your time in Finpoint.

00:06:13.405 --> 00:06:19.610
Its first tab, Allocate to Records, lists every Statement that still needs a label, newest first.

00:06:20.467 --> 00:06:23.717
Once a Statement is fully labelled, it leaves this list.

00:06:24.007 --> 00:06:28.313
So an empty list means you’re all caught up, at least until your next import.

00:06:29.167 --> 00:06:32.607
The other two tabs help you tidy up your imported Statements.

00:06:32.837 --> 00:06:34.087
We’ll cover them later.

00:06:34.967 --> 00:06:38.427
To look things up, use the Records and Statements pages.

00:06:38.697 --> 00:06:43.927
They list every Record and every Statement you have, with filters to help you find anything.

00:06:44.367 --> 00:06:45.767
Workflows is different:

00:06:45.977 --> 00:06:48.637
it only shows the Statements that still need you.

00:06:50.267 --> 00:06:52.332
One to one: your first Record

00:06:53.432 --> 00:06:56.652
Let’s label that $12 solo lunch from earlier.

00:06:56.972 --> 00:06:59.942
In Workflows, click the lunch Statement to select it.

00:07:00.272 --> 00:07:03.550
A bar will appear at the top, with what you can do next.

00:07:04.267 --> 00:07:05.707
Choose Create Record.

00:07:05.887 --> 00:07:07.807
We’ll cover the other options later.

00:07:08.237 --> 00:07:14.542
The form opens with the Statement’s $12 already allocated, and the amount and date filled in for you.

00:07:15.267 --> 00:07:19.637
Give the Record a title, like Lunch, and pick a Category, like Dining Out.

00:07:20.057 --> 00:07:24.452
A Category says what the money was for, and it will matter on your Dashboard later.

00:07:25.333 --> 00:07:29.063
Under Analytics, you’ll also see Treatment and Monthly Budget.

00:07:29.433 --> 00:07:31.303
Leave them as they are for now:

00:07:31.373 --> 00:07:33.003
your Category fills them in.

00:07:33.353 --> 00:07:35.880
They’re more advanced, so we’ll cover them later.

00:07:36.733 --> 00:07:41.303
Check that everything looks right, then choose Create Record at the bottom to save.

00:07:41.733 --> 00:07:45.639
The lunch Statement is now fully allocated, so it leaves the list.

00:07:47.267 --> 00:07:49.319
One to many: splitting a payment

00:07:50.419 --> 00:07:53.259
Now let’s split the $80 supermarket trip.

00:07:53.559 --> 00:07:56.726
Select it in Workflows, and choose Create Record.

00:07:57.433 --> 00:08:03.493
Call it Groceries, and set both the Record’s amount and its Allocation to −$50.

00:08:03.923 --> 00:08:09.683
The Record is what you spent, and the Allocation is how much of this $80 payment it uses.

00:08:10.083 --> 00:08:13.643
The other $30 stays unlabelled, for the next Record.

00:08:14.367 --> 00:08:15.857
Choose Create Record.

00:08:16.227 --> 00:08:22.723
Because only part of it is labelled, the supermarket Statement stays in the list, showing the $30 left.

00:08:23.433 --> 00:08:26.943
Select it again, and create a second Record, Birthday gift.

00:08:27.273 --> 00:08:30.397
Finpoint fills in the $30 that’s left for you.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:35.101
Choose Create Record, and the supermarket Statement leaves the list.

00:08:35.367 --> 00:08:38.647
It’s now fully labelled by the two Records we just created:

00:08:38.967 --> 00:08:42.485
$50 of groceries, and a $30 gift.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:46.286
Many to one: combining payments

00:08:47.386 --> 00:08:50.426
Now let’s combine the two payments from that shared dinner.

00:08:50.786 --> 00:08:52.346
Select both Statements:

00:08:52.596 --> 00:08:57.056
the $90 you paid, and the $60 your friend Sam sent back.

00:08:57.406 --> 00:08:58.696
The bar adds them up:

00:08:58.906 --> 00:09:03.163
$90 out and $60 in, so $30 out.

00:09:03.867 --> 00:09:06.307
Choose Create Record, and call it Dinner.

00:09:06.697 --> 00:09:11.097
Both Statements are already allocated, and the Record comes to $30:

00:09:11.247 --> 00:09:12.713
what dinner really cost you.

00:09:13.567 --> 00:09:18.007
Choose Create Record, and both Statements are labelled by a single Record.

00:09:18.437 --> 00:09:23.118
Your spending now shows the $30 you really spent, not $90.

00:09:24.733 --> 00:09:26.115
Where your numbers come from

00:09:27.215 --> 00:09:29.465
We’ve spent some time creating Records.

00:09:29.735 --> 00:09:32.095
So how does that help you understand your money?

00:09:32.405 --> 00:09:40.896
With a few Records saved, your Dashboard starts to fill in, because everything on it is calculated from Records, never directly from Statements.

00:09:41.767 --> 00:09:44.937
That’s why importing alone doesn’t change your Dashboard.

00:09:45.277 --> 00:09:48.250
A Statement only counts once a Record labels it.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:53.220
And because it uses your labels, the Dashboard shows what you really spent:

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:58.264
$30 on dinner, not $90, and groceries apart from the gift.

00:09:59.133 --> 00:10:00.983
There’s a lot more on the Dashboard.

00:10:01.173 --> 00:10:03.893
Scroll through and explore it at your own pace.

00:10:04.193 --> 00:10:06.595
We’ll come back to reading it in more depth later.

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:09.448
Pending Records

00:10:10.548 --> 00:10:13.748
Your Dashboard is only as up to date as your labels.

00:10:14.098 --> 00:10:21.573
But sometimes you know about spending before your bank shows all of it, and you’d rather label it now than try to remember it later.

00:10:22.433 --> 00:10:29.433
Say you paid $200 for two concert tickets, and your friend Jo will pay you back $100 next week.

00:10:29.843 --> 00:10:35.235
You can already create the Record for your $100 share, from the $200 payment.

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:37.650
The amounts don’t match yet:

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:40.460
$100 against $200.

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:46.220
Finpoint warns you, and saves the Record as Pending, which means it isn’t settled yet.

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:49.118
Your $100 share already counts.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:54.241
Pending Records are marked in your Records list, so nothing gets forgotten.

00:10:55.100 --> 00:11:01.140
When Jo’s $100 arrives, select it in Workflows and choose Allocate to record.

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:04.639
Finpoint will suggest the Pending Record it completes.

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:07.770
The Record now combines both payments.

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:11.852
Save it, and because the amounts add up, it’s no longer Pending.

00:11:12.733 --> 00:11:15.483
So a Record is Pending for one of two reasons.

00:11:15.753 --> 00:11:18.523
Its amounts don’t add up yet, like the concert.

00:11:18.813 --> 00:11:21.553
Or one of its Statements is still Pending at the bank.

00:11:21.923 --> 00:11:25.857
Either way, Pending means the Record isn’t fully confirmed yet.

00:11:27.467 --> 00:11:28.707
Pending Statements

00:11:29.807 --> 00:11:32.997
Remember how a Statement matches your bank, almost always?

00:11:33.277 --> 00:11:34.687
This is the exception.

00:11:34.977 --> 00:11:38.547
A Pending Statement is bank activity that isn’t confirmed yet.

00:11:38.957 --> 00:11:42.967
Some imports, like Revolut’s, bring card payments in as Pending.

00:11:43.357 --> 00:11:51.154
And if your bank takes days to show a payment, you can add one yourself, and edit it until the real one arrives, so your month stays complete.

00:11:52.033 --> 00:11:55.253
On the Statements page, choose Create Pending Statement.

00:11:55.623 --> 00:11:59.242
Pick the account, then enter the amount and a short description.

00:11:59.967 --> 00:12:02.517
It appears in Workflows, marked Pending.

00:12:02.927 --> 00:12:05.277
Label it like any other Statement:

00:12:05.347 --> 00:12:07.597
here, a burger with Mia, under Dining Out.

00:12:07.957 --> 00:12:12.867
Save it, and the Record counts straight away, but stays Pending while its Statement does.

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:19.460
When the confirmed Statement arrives in a later import, open Replace Pending in Workflows.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:25.021
Pick the Pending Statement, and Finpoint will suggest the imported Statements it could become.

00:12:25.733 --> 00:12:28.893
Review the swap, and choose Replace pending Statement.

00:12:29.313 --> 00:12:35.703
The Allocations move to the confirmed Statement, the Pending one disappears, and nothing is counted twice.

00:12:37.333 --> 00:12:39.102
Fixing duplicate Statements

00:12:40.202 --> 00:12:42.522
Imports can go wrong in one more way.

00:12:42.822 --> 00:12:46.752
Sometimes a bank changes a payment’s description between exports.

00:12:47.112 --> 00:12:52.647
Import both files, and the same payment arrives twice, so you might label it twice.

00:12:53.500 --> 00:13:00.080
Finpoint spots Statements with the same account, day and amount, and marks them as possible duplicates.

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:03.552
Open Fix Duplicates in Workflows to review them.

00:13:04.433 --> 00:13:07.603
If they really are two payments, choose Not duplicates.

00:13:07.983 --> 00:13:11.678
Otherwise, choose the Statement to keep, then Review deletion.

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:15.870
Finpoint shows you what will happen before anything is deleted.

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:20.532
Allocations follow the Statement you keep, so your Records stay labelled.

00:13:22.133 --> 00:13:23.855
Finding and fixing Records

00:13:24.955 --> 00:13:29.795
Sooner or later, you’ll want to look something up, like what that dinner with Sam really cost.

00:13:30.225 --> 00:13:35.639
Open Records, then search by title, or filter by date, Category or amount.

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:43.491
Open a Record to see its Statements, then choose Edit Record to change any field, or the Allocations behind it.

00:13:44.367 --> 00:13:50.517
The Statements page works the same way for your bank activity, and each Account has a page of its own.

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:59.463
And if you always look at the same period, set default filters in Settings, so Records and Allocate to Records open on that range.

00:14:01.067 --> 00:14:01.998
Categories

00:14:03.098 --> 00:14:05.428
We’ve been picking Categories all along.

00:14:05.828 --> 00:14:08.558
They’re the most important choice you make on a Record:

00:14:08.798 --> 00:14:12.814
they say what it was for, and your Dashboard breaks your spending down by them.

00:14:13.667 --> 00:14:16.197
Lunch and dinner with friends are Dining Out.

00:14:16.497 --> 00:14:18.767
The supermarket run is Groceries.

00:14:19.087 --> 00:14:22.097
The birthday gift goes under Gifts &amp; Donations.

00:14:22.507 --> 00:14:30.885
Finpoint starts you with a sensible set, and you can rename them, add your own, or nest one under another, like Coffee under Dining Out.

00:14:31.767 --> 00:14:34.527
Each Category also carries two defaults:

00:14:34.687 --> 00:14:36.837
a Treatment, and a Monthly Budget.

00:14:37.227 --> 00:14:40.137
That’s why you could leave those fields alone earlier:

00:14:40.207 --> 00:14:41.857
your Category fills them in.

00:14:42.147 --> 00:14:45.244
We’ll look at both, right after one more time-saver.

00:14:46.867 --> 00:14:48.022
Statement rules

00:14:49.122 --> 00:14:54.862
Some payments come back every month, like your phone bill, and labelling them again and again gets old.

00:14:55.342 --> 00:15:00.132
A Statement rule recognises them, and fills in the Record for you when you create it.

00:15:00.452 --> 00:15:02.490
It never creates Records on its own.

00:15:03.367 --> 00:15:05.467
Open Rules, and choose New rule.

00:15:05.777 --> 00:15:08.927
Give it a name, and the text to look for in the description.

00:15:09.307 --> 00:15:11.730
Finpoint shows you the Statements it matches.

00:15:12.433 --> 00:15:13.973
Then choose what it fills in:

00:15:14.153 --> 00:15:17.873
a title, a Category, even People or a Location.

00:15:18.293 --> 00:15:20.893
Autofill puts the value straight into the field.

00:15:21.173 --> 00:15:24.615
Suggest offers it as a one-click suggestion instead.

00:15:25.467 --> 00:15:33.107
Next time you create a Record from a matching Statement, the autofilled fields are ready, and the suggestions are one click away.

00:15:33.547 --> 00:15:35.274
You can still change any of them.

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:37.782
Treatments

00:15:38.882 --> 00:15:40.472
Now, those two defaults.

00:15:40.662 --> 00:15:41.982
The first is Treatment.

00:15:42.242 --> 00:15:44.632
It decides how the Dashboard counts a Record:

00:15:44.852 --> 00:15:48.501
as income, as spending, as saving, or not at all.

00:15:49.367 --> 00:15:50.947
Your salary is Income.

00:15:51.237 --> 00:15:53.417
Lunch and groceries are Spending.

00:15:53.797 --> 00:15:57.387
A refund is Spending too, because it lowers what you spent.

00:15:57.777 --> 00:16:02.617
Putting $500 into an investment account is Saving / investment.

00:16:02.897 --> 00:16:07.816
And moving money between two accounts you track is a Transfer, which isn’t counted at all.

00:16:08.667 --> 00:16:12.797
On the Dashboard, Income and Spending make up your totals and charts.

00:16:13.177 --> 00:16:19.927
Saving and investment are kept out of them, in their own section further down, so saving never looks like spending.

00:16:20.287 --> 00:16:22.172
And Transfers don’t appear at all.

00:16:23.033 --> 00:16:25.213
Your Category sets the treatment for you.

00:16:25.523 --> 00:16:29.430
Change it on a single Record only when that Record is an exception.

00:16:31.033 --> 00:16:32.369
Monthly Budgets

00:16:33.469 --> 00:16:35.729
The second default is a Monthly Budget.

00:16:35.989 --> 00:16:37.629
It answers a different question:

00:16:37.789 --> 00:16:38.999
am I on track?

00:16:39.199 --> 00:16:41.939
A Monthly Budget is a group of spending you plan for.

00:16:42.279 --> 00:16:44.039
Finpoint starts you with four:

00:16:44.219 --> 00:16:51.149
Daily for everyday spending, Recurring for bills like rent and your phone, Irregular for one-off costs, and Travel.

00:16:51.589 --> 00:16:53.825
Each gets its own amount every month.

00:16:54.700 --> 00:16:56.530
Daily is the one to watch.

00:16:56.870 --> 00:17:03.480
It holds everyday spending, like food and transport, and the Dashboard forecasts where it’s heading by the end of the month.

00:17:04.333 --> 00:17:11.263
To give Daily an amount, choose Edit beside it on the Dashboard, and set a Monthly amount, say $500.

00:17:11.967 --> 00:17:15.197
The Amount rule decides which months that amount covers.

00:17:15.537 --> 00:17:18.317
Override one month changes this month only.

00:17:18.657 --> 00:17:21.595
Override default applies from this month onward.

00:17:22.467 --> 00:17:25.987
The Dashboard then shows how the month is going against the plan.

00:17:26.327 --> 00:17:29.347
A Monthly Budget doesn’t move or set aside any money.

00:17:29.707 --> 00:17:31.865
It’s a comparison, nothing more.

00:17:33.467 --> 00:17:34.782
Reading the Overview

00:17:35.882 --> 00:17:41.132
With a plan in place, the Overview, the Dashboard’s first tab, becomes your monthly check-in.

00:17:41.512 --> 00:17:43.702
Its top answers the big questions:

00:17:43.952 --> 00:17:47.862
what came in, what you spent, and your surplus, which is the difference.

00:17:48.312 --> 00:17:52.628
Each number is compared with the last two months, at the same point in the month.

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:57.700
Choose a Monthly Budget at the top, and the spending charts narrow to it.

00:17:58.090 --> 00:18:03.018
Cards whose names start with Total ignore that choice, and always show everything.

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:11.767
The Spending forecast chart shows this month so far, where it’s heading, and the months before, so you can see early if you’re running ahead.

00:18:12.633 --> 00:18:19.821
Further down, Categories this month and their trend show what changed, and Biggest changes calls out the largest moves.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:23.600
The calendar colours each day by how much you spent.

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:25.605
Pick a day to see its Records.

00:18:27.233 --> 00:18:28.570
Monthly Records

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:34.460
When a number on the Overview surprises you, Monthly Records shows what’s behind it.

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:36.540
It sits next to the Overview:

00:18:36.690 --> 00:18:40.700
every Record in the month, grouped by day, with each day’s total.

00:18:41.567 --> 00:18:47.218
Click any part of a chart on the Overview, and Monthly Records opens on just those Records.

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:52.490
And at the bottom of the Overview, Review this month links to anything that needs you:

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:59.572
Pending Records, Pending Statements, and spending that isn’t in any Monthly Budget, so no plan is tracking it.

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:02.961
Budgets for trips and events

00:19:04.061 --> 00:19:06.341
Everything so far works month by month.

00:19:06.681 --> 00:19:09.701
A Budget, with no Monthly in front, is different:

00:19:10.001 --> 00:19:14.221
it has its own start and end, like a week in Bali, or a wedding.

00:19:14.611 --> 00:19:18.394
You see the whole trip in one place, even when it spans two months.

00:19:19.267 --> 00:19:22.155
Create one with a name, an amount and its dates.

00:19:22.867 --> 00:19:28.987
Automatic attach adds every Record dated inside the period, including income and transfers.

00:19:29.407 --> 00:19:34.141
To choose the Records yourself, turn it off, and attach them from the Budget’s page.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:38.120
A Budget is its own small dashboard for that period:

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:41.140
what you’ve spent, what’s left, and your pace.

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:44.794
Its Records still count on your monthly Dashboard as usual.

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:47.994
Backups and Google Drive

00:19:49.094 --> 00:19:53.464
You’ve put real work into labelling your money, so it’s worth knowing where it lives.

00:19:53.884 --> 00:19:57.934
Finpoint runs in your browser, and keeps your data on this device.

00:19:58.324 --> 00:20:04.210
There’s no Finpoint database, and your finances never pass through our servers, so we never see them.

00:20:05.067 --> 00:20:08.687
That means keeping a copy is up to you, and there are two ways.

00:20:09.087 --> 00:20:10.847
The first is a backup file.

00:20:11.037 --> 00:20:17.137
Download it from the Data page whenever you like, and restore it later, on this device or a new one.

00:20:17.577 --> 00:20:20.944
Restoring replaces this browser’s data with the backup.

00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:23.650
The second is Google Drive.

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:31.732
Connect it once, and Finpoint keeps a copy in a hidden folder in your own Drive, automatically, so your devices stay in step.

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:42.658
If the same data changed on two devices, Finpoint asks which copy to keep, and keeps that whole copy, never a mix of both, so your Records always add up.

00:20:43.533 --> 00:20:46.912
Either way, your data only goes to places you control.

00:20:48.533 --> 00:20:49.556
Your routine

00:20:50.656 --> 00:20:52.376
Here’s a routine that works.

00:20:52.546 --> 00:20:54.676
Import your latest bank exports.

00:20:54.876 --> 00:20:57.846
Fix any duplicates, and label what’s new in Workflows.

00:20:58.116 --> 00:20:59.476
Check anything Pending.

00:20:59.736 --> 00:21:01.186
Glance at your Dashboard.

00:21:01.266 --> 00:21:02.400
And keep a backup.

00:21:03.267 --> 00:21:06.647
With Google Drive connected, that happens automatically.

00:21:07.027 --> 00:21:10.147
If not, download a backup file every so often.

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:14.240
And whenever you’re unsure, choose Help at the top of any page.

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:17.674
It lists the guides for that page, step by step.
